<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="skill">enchanting</ansi> (skill)

Enchantments are not forged -- they are grown. Living Chrysalis
mutations bind themselves to your equipment through crafted settings,
growing stronger with use. The skill is governed by
<ansi fg="yellow">Perception</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ How Enchantments Work ━━━</ansi>

A Chrysalis enchantment feeds on its wearer. You will see a dark
section in your vital bars -- that part of the pool belongs to the
enchantment now, for as long as you wear the item. It is not damage
and rest will not return it. As you fight with the enchanted item
equipped, the bond deepens and may advance to a higher tier.

A deeper tier feeds more. Two-handed weapons receive doubled
enchantment effects, and feed twice as deeply for them.

There is a limit to how much of any pool may be held in reserve at
once, counting your gear and your companions together. An enchantment
will not deepen further when you have nothing left to give it, and a
new one will be refused rather than quietly leaving you weaker. The
refusal costs you nothing.

A skilled enchanter binds magic that takes less from its wearer, so
raising the skill widens what you can carry as surely as it improves
what you can make.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Requirements ━━━</ansi>

  Station:   An <ansi fg="yellow">enchanting circle</ansi> in the room.
  Materials: Binding paste, chrysalis settings, and catalysts.
  Target:    The item must be <ansi fg="yellow">equipped</ansi> (not in your backpack).

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Targeting ━━━</ansi>

Enchanting targets your equipped gear by slot name:

  <ansi fg="command">craft honed-edge</ansi>           Enchants your wielded weapon.
  <ansi fg="command">craft honed-edge weapon#2</ansi>   Enchants your offhand weapon.
  <ansi fg="command">craft thornguard shield</ansi>     Enchants your shield.
  <ansi fg="command">craft ironblood ring#2</ansi>      Enchants your second ring.
  <ansi fg="command">craft chitin-brace 2.wrist</ansi>  Enchants your second wrist slot.

For slots with only one item (head, neck, body, etc.), just use
the recipe name, with no slot specifier needed.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Enchantment Recipes ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Weapon Enchants:</ansi>
  Honed-Edge, Serpents-Edge, Hungering-Touch

  <ansi fg="yellow">Shield Enchant:</ansi>
  Thornguard

  <ansi fg="yellow">Armor Enchants:</ansi>
  Carapace-Ward (body), Sporeweave (body), Chitin-Brace (wrist),
  Rootwalker (feet), Spore-Mantle (shoulders), Shadowweave (back),
  Rootbind (belt), Venomgrip (gloves)

  <ansi fg="yellow">Accessory Enchants:</ansi>
  Chrysalis-Sight (head), Mindweave (head), Ironblood (neck),
  Chrysalis-Stride (legs), Predators-Instinct (ring),
  Chrysalis-Bond (ring)

Type <ansi fg="command">help <recipe-name></ansi> for details on any recipe.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Commands ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">craft list</ansi>          Show all recipes with status.
  <ansi fg="command">craft <recipe></ansi>      Begin enchanting an item.
  <ansi fg="command">disenchant <item></ansi>   Remove an enchantment from an equipped item.
  <ansi fg="command">skills</ansi>              Check your current enchanting skill level.

<ansi fg="yellow">See Also:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">help craft</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help disenchant</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="command">help reservation</ansi>
